.TH CPOP 1 "September 2026" "cpop 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpop \- run a command and copy its output to the system clipboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpop
[\fIFLAGS\fR]
.I command
[\fIargs\fR...]
.br
.B cpop
.B \-h
|
.B \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpop
(copy output) runs
.I command
with the given arguments, shows its output on the terminal as usual, and copies the same output to the system clipboard.
Standard output and standard error are captured together.
ANSI color codes are removed from the copy unless
.B \-r
is given.
The exit status of
.I command
is passed through unchanged, so
.B cpop
can be put in front of a command inside scripts and
.B &&
chains.
.PP
When the copy succeeds, a one-line banner is printed to standard error showing how many lines and bytes were copied and which format was used.
If the command prints nothing, the clipboard is left untouched.
.PP
Shell pipelines and redirections are handled by the shell before
.B cpop
starts, so quote them and run them inside a shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cpop sh \-c 'ls | wc \-l'
.fi
.RE
.SH FORMAT FLAGS
These flags change only what is placed on the clipboard.
The terminal always shows the command's raw output.
Only one of
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-m ,
.B \-j
and
.B \-p
may be used at a time.
They require
.BR python3 (1).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
Aligned, box-drawn table.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-markdown
Markdown table, ready to paste into GitHub, Slack or Notion.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
JSON.
Tabular output becomes an array of objects keyed by the header row, other text becomes an array of lines, and output that is already JSON is pretty-printed.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty
Best guess: pretty-printed JSON if the output is JSON, otherwise a box-drawn table.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-code
Wrap the copy in a Markdown code fence.
May be combined with any of the flags above.
.SH OTHER FLAGS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-raw
Keep ANSI escape sequences in the copy.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print the banner.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Short flags may be bundled, for example
.BR "cpop \-mc df \-h" .
Use
.B \-\-
to end flag parsing if the command name starts with a dash.
Flags placed after
.I command
belong to that command.
.SH TABLES
Table formats split each line on whitespace.
The column count is the most common word count among the data rows, extra words stay in the last column, and the first full-width line is the header.
This suits
.BR df (1),
.BR ps (1),
.B docker ps
and similar tools.
Commands without a header row, such as
.BR "ls \-l" ,
use their first row as the header, and columns that contain spaces are split.
If the output is not tabular,
.B cpop
copies it unchanged and says so.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CPOP_QUIET
If set and non-empty, behave as if
.B \-q
was given.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, the banner is printed without colors.
Colors are also off when standard error is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY
Used to pick the clipboard backend that fits the current session.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
On non-UTF-8 locales the banner and table borders use plain ASCII.
.SH CLIPBOARD BACKENDS
The first tool that fits the session is used:
.BR wl\-copy ,
.BR xclip ,
.BR xsel ,
.BR pbcopy ,
.BR clip.exe .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.IR command .
.B cpop
itself exits with 2 on a usage error, 127 if
.I command
or a required tool cannot be found, and 1 if the clipboard cannot be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cpop ls \-a
cpop \-m df \-h
cpop \-j ps aux
cpop \-pc curl \-s https://api.github.com/zen
cpop \-t sh \-c 'ls | head'
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xclip (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1),
.BR tee (1)
